    "created_at": "2017-02-03T06:52:32Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-02-03T06:52:32Z",
    "body": "Talk about a quick response! This isn't so much about the protocol itself, it's actually very URL-intrinsic.\r\n\r\nSo here's where this comes up. As you know, URLs with a scheme that uses a network location get a '//' whereas those without do not. `http://github.com` vs `mailto:mahmoud@hatnote.com`. \r\n\r\nIf the standard is enforced strictly, and the scheme is treated as a whole, it's not possible to guess if a scheme implies the URL has a network location. So we don't know whether it should be `git+ftp://...` or `git+ftp:...`, despite the fact that you or I can clearly guess.\r\n\r\nA manifestation of this can be seen in Python's built-in URL support, here: https://hg.python.org/cpython/file/2.7/Lib/urlparse.py#l41",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/url/issues/comments/277177423/reactions",
